{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"idea-generation-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Idea generation track","description":"Takes a challenge from framing to a tested idea in five paused steps - frame the question, diverge with two techniques, converge, develop the pick, and plan a cheap test.","category":"brainstorming","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan"],"role":["individual","manager","founder","product-manager"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["topic","text"],"output":["ideas","plan"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["creative-problem-solving","divergent-convergent","idea-to-test","ideation-process"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-how-might-we-questions","cluster-ideas","develop-idea-with-ppco","validate-business-idea"],"personas":["ideation-partner"]},"args":[{"name":"challenge","description":"What you need ideas for, why it matters, constraints (money, time, people, rules), what has been tried and who decides.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"frame","file":"steps/01-frame.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve"},{"id":"diverge","file":"steps/02-diverge.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve"},{"id":"converge","file":"steps/03-converge.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"develop","file":"steps/04-develop.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"test-plan","file":"steps/05-test-plan.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Guides a person from a challenge to one developed idea and a cheap test, one step at a time, pausing after each step for their reply. It follows the Creative Problem Solving rhythm: diverge then converge at every stage, and never judge while generating. The person owns every choice; the assistant frames, generates alongside them, builds on their ideas before adding its own, and keeps plans within their constraints. It never invents facts, prices, rules or research about the person's situation, and labels assumptions.\n\n<challenge>\n{{challenge}}\n</challenge>\n\nIf the challenge involves harming, deceiving or excluding people, do not run the track; say so briefly and offer to work on the underlying need. If the person asks to skip the pauses, confirm once that later steps will build on unconfirmed choices; if they agree, run the remaining steps in one reply and mark each choice made for them.","steps":[{"id":"frame","file":"steps/01-frame.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Frame the question\n\nA good question is half the work.\n\n1. Restate the challenge in one sentence, then list the constraints, what has been tried and who decides. Mark anything you inferred.\n2. Write five to seven \"How might we ...?\" questions at different levels: some broader (the goal behind the goal), some narrower (one part of the problem), and one that flips the problem.\n3. Flag questions that are a disguised single solution (\"How might we build an app ...\") or too broad to act on.\n4. Recommend one or two questions to carry forward and say why.\n\nStop and ask: \"Which question should we work on, or would you reword one?\""},{"id":"diverge","file":"steps/02-diverge.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Diverge\n\nGenerate a wide range before judging any of it.\n\n1. Ask the person for their own first ideas, however rough, if they have not given any; include them first and build on them.\n2. Pick two techniques that suit the question and say why, for example: reverse brainstorming for a stuck service problem, SCAMPER for an existing product or process, analogies from other fields for a new concept, random words when thinking is stuck in a rut, assumption reversal for a familiar industry.\n3. Generate 30 to 40 ideas across both techniques, one line each, numbered, grouped by technique. Push past the obvious: at least a third should feel surprising.\n4. Do not evaluate yet. No ratings, no \"but\".\n\nStop and ask: \"Add any ideas these spark, star the ones that interest you, then we will narrow down.\""},{"id":"converge","file":"steps/03-converge.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Converge\n\nNarrow down transparently, without losing bold ideas.\n\n1. Merge duplicates and cluster the ideas into five to eight named themes, keeping every idea number traceable.\n2. Agree criteria with the person: suggest three or four drawn from the constraints and goal (for example impact on the goal, ease within the budget, originality, fit with who decides), and let them change them.\n3. Score the person's starred ideas plus the strongest from each cluster against the criteria, with one-line reasons.\n4. Show a shortlist of three to five, deliberately including at least one original idea alongside quick wins.\n\nStop and ask: \"Which one idea should we develop? You can also combine two.\""},{"id":"develop","file":"steps/04-develop.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Develop the chosen idea\n\nStrengthen the idea before anyone tries to kill it.\n\n1. Describe the idea in three to five sentences: what it is, who it is for, how it works.\n2. Run PPCo: pluses (specific strengths now), potentials (what it could lead to), concerns phrased as \"How to ...?\" questions, then two or three ways to overcome each of the top concerns.\n3. Rewrite the idea with the chosen fixes. If a concern cannot be overcome, say so plainly and suggest going back to the shortlist.\n4. List the assumptions the idea depends on and mark the riskiest one: the belief that, if wrong, sinks the idea.\n\nStop and ask: \"Does this version feel right, and is that the riskiest assumption?\""},{"id":"test-plan","file":"steps/05-test-plan.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Plan a cheap test\n\nLearn whether the idea works before investing in it.\n\n1. Design the cheapest test of the riskiest assumption that can run within two weeks: a conversation with a set number of the people it is for, a mock-up, a one-off trial, a sign-up page or a manual version.\n2. Set the success threshold before the test runs, and what happens if it is met, missed or unclear.\n3. List what the test needs: people, materials, permissions to check, and who does what by when.\n4. Close with a one-paragraph summary of the journey: the question, the idea, why it was chosen and the test.\n\nRemind the person they can rerun step 2 with different techniques if the test fails."}]}
